Bootstrap: docker
From: ubuntu:22.04

%labels
    Maintainer sdweave2@ncsu.edu
    Source https://github.com/EI-CoreBioinformatics/portcullis
    Version 1.2.4_patch

%help
    portcullis — filters spurious splice junctions from pre-aligned
    RNA-seq alignments using a self-trained random forest (or a custom
    rule-based filter).

    THIS IS A LOCALLY-PATCHED SOURCE BUILD, not vanilla upstream 1.2.4.
    Re-pulling the plain Release-1.2.4 tag (or the bioconda/biocontainers
    package of the same version) will NOT carry this fix — the patch
    below is BRC's own, not upstream's.

    Deployed as a SEPARATE version (1.2.4_patch) alongside the existing
    portcullis/1.2.4 module (the unpatched, broken-filter build) on
    purpose — this build has not yet had broad user testing, and the
    existing 1.2.4 module is left completely untouched so nothing
    currently depending on it is disrupted. Load explicitly with
    `module load portcullis/1.2.4_patch`.

    Both the official quay.io/biocontainers image for this version
    (1.2.4--py310hfc0ef84_5) AND an independent from-scratch rebuild via
    bioconda (mamba install portcullis=1.2.4, no Docker layering at all)
    reliably crash during `portcullis filter` with:
        Error: basic_string: construction from null is not valid
    on BOTH the default self-trained ML filter and rule-based (-f)
    filtering, on every real dataset tried, before any output from
    portcullis's embedded Python step appears (it links libpython
    directly and calls into it in-process; confirmed via strace that no
    execve to a python3 process ever happens). Since the bug reproduces
    identically across two independently-built binaries of the exact
    same upstream source, it is not a container-packaging artifact.

    Root cause, found by reading bioconda's own patch set for this
    package: bioconda's 0004-python_helper.patch (needed to fix a
    build-time API removal in newer CPython) changed
    lib/include/portcullis/python_helper.hpp's Py_Initialize() call to
    the modern Py_InitializeFromConfig()/PyConfig API, but left the
    *other* calls in that same constructor as the OLD legacy globals
    (Py_SetProgramName, Py_GetPath, Py_SetPath) -- CPython does not
    support mixing the legacy init globals with a separate PyConfig
    handed to Py_InitializeFromConfig() afterwards. This build instead
    rewrites that constructor to use only the modern PyConfig API end to
    end (see the inline patch applied in %post below for the exact
    diff/rationale). This has NOT yet been proposed upstream.

    BUILD NOTE: this source build also hit a second, separate bug during
    ./configure -- Ubuntu 22.04 ships Python 3.10, whose stdlib
    'distutils' module prints a DeprecationWarning to stderr on import.
    autoconf-archive's AX_PYTHON_DEVEL macro (used by portcullis's
    configure.ac to detect distutils) treats ANY stderr output from its
    `python3 -c "import distutils"` probe as an import failure, even
    though the import itself succeeds -- so configure aborted with
    "Python3 not detected" despite python3/python3-dev/python3-distutils
    all being correctly installed. bioconda's own 0002-ax_python_devel.patch
    (applied below) does not cover this particular check. Fixed here by
    exporting PYTHONWARNINGS=ignore::DeprecationWarning for the duration
    of the build so the macro's probe sees clean output; this has no
    effect on portcullis's runtime behavior.

    VERIFIED (BRC/Hazel, 2026-09-18): both the default self-trained RF
    filter and rule-based (-f) filtering ran to completion against real
    junction data (673,505 input junctions; self-trained mode passed
    530,019, rule-based mode also completed cleanly) -- exit code 0,
    normal pass/fail .tab/.bed/.intron.gff3 outputs written, no crash.
    The built-in test step below still only proves the binary launches
    and parses arguments (--help), since exercising the embedded Python
    init path requires real prep/junc input data not available at build
    time -- the verification above was done separately against a real
    dataset.

    Usage:
        apptainer run portcullis-1.2.4_patch.sif <prep|junc|filter|full> [options]

    Common subcommands:
        prep    Prepares BAM and genome for junction analysis
        junc    Identifies potential junctions from a prepared BAM
        filter  Filters junctions (self-trained RF by default; -f/--filter_file
                for a custom rule-based JSON filter; -n/--no_ml to disable ML)
        full    Runs prep + junc + filter in one go

    Example:
        apptainer run portcullis-1.2.4_patch.sif filter --threads 4 \
            --output portcullis_filter/portcullis --save_bad --intron_gff \
            <prep_dir> <junctions.tab>

%post -c /bin/bash
    set -e
    set -o pipefail
    # The header above MUST read exactly '%post -c /bin/bash' — Apptainer's
    # %post does NOT honor a '#!/bin/bash' shebang line inside the body;
    # without -c /bin/bash it runs under /bin/sh (dash), which lacks
    # 'set -o pipefail'. Apptainer also does not fail the build on a
    # failed %post command without 'set -e'.
    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive

    # Works around a known autoconf-archive AX_PYTHON_DEVEL bug: on
    # Python 3.10+, `import distutils` prints a DeprecationWarning to
    # stderr, and the macro's probe treats any stderr output as an
    # import failure -- aborting configure with "Python3 not detected"
    # even though distutils imports fine. Scoped to DeprecationWarning
    # only so real errors elsewhere still surface. Must be set before
    # autoreconf/configure run (both invoke python3 subprocesses).
    export PYTHONWARNINGS="ignore::DeprecationWarning"

    # Hazel bind-mount points
    mkdir -p /rs1 /share /home /usr/local/usrapps

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Build dependencies (Pattern 0/2 hybrid: adapting upstream's own
    # autotools build, translated from bioconda's build.sh onto a plain
    # Ubuntu base instead of conda, specifically so we can apply our own
    # patch to lib/include/portcullis/python_helper.hpp — see %help).
    #
    # python3-distutils is required in addition to python3-dev: on
    # Ubuntu 22.04, Debian/Ubuntu split the stdlib 'distutils' module out
    # of the base python3 package into this separate package even though
    # CPython 3.10 itself still ships it. Without it, configure's
    # AX_PYTHON_DEVEL check ("checking for the distutils Python
    # package...") fails to import distutils and configure aborts with
    # "Python3 not detected" even though python3/python3-dev/python3-pip
    # are all present and correct.
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    apt-get update -qq && \
        apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
            build-essential \
            autoconf \
            automake \
            libtool \
            pkg-config \
            m4 \
            libboost-all-dev \
            zlib1g-dev \
            samtools \
            python3 \
            python3-dev \
            python3-distutils \
            python3-pip \
            ca-certificates \
            curl && \
        apt-get clean && \
        rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

    # meta.yaml pins setuptools<82 (newer setuptools removed something
    # scripts/{junctools,portcullis}/setup.py needs); pandas/numpy/
    # matplotlib/tabulate/sphinx match bioconda's host+run requirements
    # (pandas is also required at ./configure time -- AX_PYTHON_MODULE
    # checks it's importable).
    pip3 install --no-cache-dir "setuptools<82" pandas numpy matplotlib tabulate sphinx

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Fetch upstream source (authoritative -- same tag bioconda builds:
    # EI-CoreBioinformatics/portcullis, Release-1.2.4).
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    mkdir -p /opt/build
    cd /opt/build
    curl -fsSL -o portcullis.tar.gz \
        https://github.com/EI-CoreBioinformatics/portcullis/archive/refs/tags/Release-1.2.4.tar.gz
    tar xzf portcullis.tar.gz
    cd portcullis-Release-1.2.4

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Apply bioconda's own compatibility patches (boost API renames for
    # newer boost, an ax_python_devel.m4 version-check fix, and dropping
    # -m64 which isn't needed/valid on all targets). These three are
    # upstream-agnostic compile fixes, unrelated to the actual bug, and
    # are adopted as-is.
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    for p in 0001-boost-leaf.patch 0002-ax_python_devel.patch 0003-src.patch m64.patch; do
        curl -fsSL -o "/tmp/${p}" \
            "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/bioconda/bioconda-recipes/master/recipes/portcullis/${p}"
        patch -p1 < "/tmp/${p}"
    done

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # BRC fix: replace python_helper.hpp with a fully PyConfig-based
    # rewrite of PyHelper's constructor (see %help for the diagnosis).
    # This supersedes bioconda's own 0004-python_helper.patch -- keeps
    # its _Py_fopen -> fopen fix (still needed to compile against
    # newer CPython) but rewrites the interpreter-init sequence instead
    # of layering the new PyConfig call on top of the old legacy globals.
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    cat > lib/include/portcullis/python_helper.hpp << 'PYHELPEREOF'
//  ********************************************************************
//  This file is part of Portcullis.
//
//  Portcullis is free software: you can redistribute it and/or modify
//  it under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by
//  the Free Software Foundation, either version 3 of the License, or
//  (at your option) any later version.
//
//  Portcullis is distributed in the hope that it will be useful,
//  but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY; without even the implied warranty of
//  MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE.  See the
//  GNU General Public License for more details.
//
//  You should have received a copy of the GNU General Public License
//  along with Portcullis.  If not, see <http://www.gnu.org/licenses/>.
//  *******************************************************************

#pragma once

#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <sstream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>
using std::stringstream;
using std::ifstream;
using std::string;
using std::cout;
using std::endl;
using std::vector;

#ifdef HAVE_PYTHON
#include <Python.h>
#endif

#include <boost/filesystem/path.hpp>
#include <boost/algorithm/string/join.hpp>
namespace bfs = boost::filesystem;
using bfs::path;

#include <portcullis/portcullis_fs.hpp>

namespace portcullis {

typedef boost::error_info<struct PortcullisPythonError,string> PortcullisPythonErrorInfo;
struct PortcullisPythonException: virtual boost::exception, virtual std::exception { };

// This class provides an easy way to run embedded python scripts within Portcullis.
// This is a singleton class as the python API can be a bit tetchy, at least the
// way we use it here.  So only one global version of this should be available.
class PyHelper {
private:
    bool verbose;
    wchar_t* py_interp = NULL;

    string full_python_path_str;
    wchar_t* full_python_path_wchar = NULL;

    PyHelper() {
#ifdef PY_DEBUG
        this->verbose = true;
#else
		this->verbose = false;
#endif

#ifdef HAVE_PYTHON

        if (this->verbose) {
            cout << endl << "Initialising python interpreter ..." << endl;
        }

        // BRC fix (2026-09-18): rewritten to use ONLY the modern PyConfig
        // init API end to end. The upstream/bioconda version mixed legacy
        // globals (Py_SetProgramName, Py_GetPath, Py_SetPath) with a later
        // PyConfig/Py_InitializeFromConfig call that bioconda's own
        // 0004-python_helper.patch added just to fix a build-time API
        // removal in newer CPython. Py_GetPath() implicitly triggers
        // CPython's legacy pre-init path calculation, and that state is
        // NOT shared with a separate, fresh PyConfig handed to
        // Py_InitializeFromConfig() afterwards -- CPython does not
        // support mixing the two initialization styles. Reproduced
        // (BRC/Hazel, 2026-09-18): this exact sequence crashes with
        // "basic_string: construction from null is not valid" before any
        // embedded python code runs, identically on the official
        // biocontainers image AND an independent from-source rebuild --
        // i.e. this is a bug in the init sequence itself, not a packaging
        // artifact.
        //
        // Fix: never call the legacy setters/getters at all. Set
        // config.program_name to PYTHON_INT_PATH (the real python3
        // interpreter configure detected -- see m4/ax_python_devel.m4)
        // so CPython's own path-finder can correctly locate its stdlib
        // relative to that interpreter, and set config.pythonpath_env to
        // just portcullis's own extra directories (its scripts dir +
        // site-packages) instead of re-injecting a stale pre-init
        // snapshot of the baseline sys.path.
        this->py_interp = Py_DecodeLocale(PYTHON_INT_PATH, NULL);

        string py_path_here(portcullis::pfs.getScriptsDir().string() + ":" + PYTHON_INTERP_SITE_PKGS);
        this->full_python_path_str = py_path_here;
        if (this->verbose) {
            cout << " - Program name (python interpreter) : " << PYTHON_INT_PATH << endl
                 << " - PYTHONPATH (added here)           : " << py_path_here << endl;
        }
        this->full_python_path_wchar = Py_DecodeLocale(this->full_python_path_str.c_str(), NULL);

        PyConfig config;
        PyConfig_InitPythonConfig(&config);
        PyStatus status;

        status = PyConfig_SetString(&config, &config.program_name, this->py_interp);
        if (PyStatus_Exception(status)) {
            PyConfig_Clear(&config);
            BOOST_THROW_EXCEPTION(PortcullisPythonException() << PortcullisPythonErrorInfo(string(
                    "Failed to set python interpreter program_name")));
        }

        status = PyConfig_SetString(&config, &config.pythonpath_env, this->full_python_path_wchar);
        if (PyStatus_Exception(status)) {
            PyConfig_Clear(&config);
            BOOST_THROW_EXCEPTION(PortcullisPythonException() << PortcullisPythonErrorInfo(string(
                    "Failed to set python interpreter pythonpath_env")));
        }

        status = Py_InitializeFromConfig(&config);
        PyConfig_Clear(&config);
        if (PyStatus_Exception(status)) {
            BOOST_THROW_EXCEPTION(PortcullisPythonException() << PortcullisPythonErrorInfo(string(
                    "Failed to initialise embedded python interpreter")));
        }

        if (this->verbose) {
            cout << "Python interpretter initialised" << endl << endl;
        }
#else
        BOOST_THROW_EXCEPTION(PortcullisPythonException() << PortcullisPythonErrorInfo(string(
                "Portcullis not configured to use Python.  Please check your configuration and rebuild.")));
#endif
    }

public:

    PyHelper(PyHelper const&)        = delete;
    void operator=(PyHelper const&)  = delete;

    static PyHelper& getInstance() {
        static PyHelper instance; // Guaranteed to be destroyed.  Instantiated on first use.
        return instance;
    }

    void execute(const string script_name, int argc, char *argv[]) {
#ifdef HAVE_PYTHON
        if (this->verbose) {
            cout << "Executing python script: " << script_name << " ..." << endl;
        }

        const path scripts_dir = portcullis::pfs.getScriptsDir();
        const path full_script_path = path(scripts_dir.string() + "/" + script_name);

        stringstream ss;

        // Create wide char alternatives
        wchar_t* wsn = Py_DecodeLocale(script_name.c_str(), NULL);
        wchar_t* wsp = Py_DecodeLocale(full_script_path.c_str(), NULL);
        wchar_t* wargv[50]; // Can't use variable length arrays!
        wargv[0] = wsp;
        ss << full_script_path.c_str();
        for(int i = 1; i < argc; i++) {
            wargv[i] = Py_DecodeLocale(argv[i], NULL);
            ss << " " << argv[i];
        }
        for(int i = argc; i < 50; i++) {
            wargv[i] = Py_DecodeLocale("\0", NULL);
        }
        if (this->verbose) {
            cout << " - Setting arguments" << endl;
        }
        PySys_SetArgv(argc, wargv);

        // BRC fix: _Py_fopen is a CPython-internal symbol no longer
        // exported by newer CPython builds (matches bioconda's own
        // 0004-python_helper.patch for this line) -- use plain fopen.
        FILE* pf = fopen(full_script_path.c_str(), "r");
        if (pf == NULL) {
            BOOST_THROW_EXCEPTION(PortcullisPythonException() << PortcullisPythonErrorInfo(string(
                    "Could not open script file as a python file object") + full_script_path.string()));
        }

        if (this->verbose) {
            cout << " - Effective command line: python3 " << ss.str() << endl;
            cout << " - Output from python script follows: " << endl << endl;
        }

        if (PyRun_SimpleFileEx(pf, full_script_path.c_str(), true) != 0) {
            BOOST_THROW_EXCEPTION(PortcullisPythonException() << PortcullisPythonErrorInfo(string(
                    "Unexpected python error")));
        }

        // Cleanup
        PyMem_RawFree(wsn);
        // No need to free up "wsp" as it is element 0 in the array
        for(int i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
            PyMem_RawFree(wargv[i]);
        }

        if (this->verbose) {
            cout << endl << "Python script \"" << script_name << "\" executed successfully" << endl;
        }
#else
        BOOST_THROW_EXCEPTION(PortcullisPythonException() << PortcullisPythonErrorInfo(string(
                "Portcullis not configured to use Python.  Please check your configuration and rebuild.")));
#endif
    }

    ~PyHelper() {
#ifdef HAVE_PYTHON
        Py_Finalize();
        PyMem_RawFree(this->py_interp);
        PyMem_RawFree(this->full_python_path_wchar);
#endif
    }
};

}
PYHELPEREOF

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Build (autotools) -- mirrors bioconda's build.sh flags exactly,
    # minus the gnuconfig config.guess/config.sub refresh (Ubuntu 22.04's
    # own autoconf package already ships a current enough version for a
    # standard x86_64-pc-linux-gnu build).
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    export CXXFLAGS="-O2 -std=c++14"
    export ax_cv_python_version_ok=yes
    autoreconf -if
    ./configure --enable-silent-rules \
        --disable-dependency-tracking \
        --disable-option-checking \
        --prefix=/usr/local \
        --with-boost=/usr \
        --with-boost-libdir=/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu
    make -j"$(nproc)"

    # setuptools>=82 dropped find_packages in favour of
    # find_namespace_packages-only paths -- same fix bioconda applies.
    sed -i 's|find_packages|find_namespace_packages|' scripts/junctools/setup.py scripts/portcullis/setup.py

    (cd scripts/junctools && pip3 install . --no-build-isolation --no-deps --no-cache-dir)
    (cd scripts/portcullis && pip3 install . --no-build-isolation --no-deps --no-cache-dir)

    make install
    strip /usr/local/bin/portcullis

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Cleanup
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    cd /
    rm -rf /opt/build

%environment
    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive

%runscript
    exec portcullis "$@"

%test
    # portcullis's top-level --help is not confirmed to exit 0 under its
    # CLI parser, so verify on output content rather than trusting the
    # exit code. This only proves the binary launches and parses
    # arguments (--help), since exercising the embedded Python init path
    # this rebuild targets requires real prep/junc input data not
    # available at build time -- see %help for the real-data verification.
    portcullis --help 2>&1 | grep -qi "portcullis"
